[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **complete, minimal example** that shows how to

1. **Load a PMTiles file** (a single-file container for vector tiles) with the official `@protomaps/pmtiles` library.  
2. **Add it to a MapLibre GL JS v6 map** as a vector source.  
3. **Create a line layer** that draws a line-type feature from that source.

The code uses **ESM-only imports** (the only build that exists in v6) and works both from a CDN and from a bundler-based project – just swap the import URLs if you’re using a bundler.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML + ESM entry point

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>PMTiles → MapLibre line layer</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Imports (CDN version – replace with local paths for a bundler)
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles, registerProtocol } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.mjs';

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Create the MapLibre map
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add our PMTiles source later
      layers: []            // we’ll add the line layer later
    },
    center: [0, 0],         // lon, lat
    zoom: 2
  });

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Register the `pmtiles://` protocol handler
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // The PMTiles library works by intercepting fetches that start with
  // the custom protocol `pmtiles://`.  We give the handler a name
  // (here “mytiles”) that we’ll later use in the source URL.
  const pmtilesUrl = 'https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-file.pmtiles'; // <-- change this
  const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
  registerProtocol(pm);   // registers under the name derived from the URL

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add the vector source that points at the PMTiles file
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // The source URL must use the `pmtiles://` protocol followed by the
  // *exact* name that the PMTiles instance registered.  The name is the
  // part after the last slash of the URL (without the .pmtiles suffix).
  // Example:  https://example.com/roads.pmtiles  →  protocol name = "roads"
  const sourceName = pmtilesUrl.split('/').pop().replace(/\.pmtiles$/, '');
  map.addSource(sourceName, {
    type: 'vector',
    // The protocol handler makes the URL look like a normal tile URL.
    // MapLibre will request tiles like: pmtiles://roads/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
    url: `pmtiles://${sourceName}`
  });

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Add a line layer that draws from the source
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // You need to know the *source-layer* name that lives inside the
  // vector tiles.  You can discover it with tools like `pmtiles info`,
  // `tile-join --list`, or by inspecting the tiles in QGIS/Mapbox Studio.
  const sourceLayerName = 'roads';   // <-- change to the actual layer name

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-line-layer',
    type: 'line',
    source: sourceName,
    'source-layer': sourceLayerName,
    layout: {
      'line-cap': 'round',
      'line-join': 'round'
    },
    paint: {
      // Example styling – feel free to tweak
      'line-color': '#ff6600',
      'line-width': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],
        ['zoom'],
        5, 0.5,
        12, 2,
        18, 8
      ],
      'line-opacity': 0.9
    }
  });

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  Optional: fit the map to the data bounds once the source loads
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('data', (e) => {
    if (e.sourceId === sourceName && e.dataType === 'source' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
      const bounds = map.getSource(sourceName).bounds; // works if the source has a `bounds` property
      if (bounds) map.fitBounds(bounds, { padding: 20 });
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Imports** | `maplibre-gl` v6 is imported as an ES module (`*.mjs`). The PMTiles helper (`@protomaps/pmtiles`) is also an ES module. |
| **Map creation** | A bare-bones style object is passed; we’ll inject the source & layer later. |
| **Protocol registration** | `registerProtocol(pm)` tells the browser’s fetch system to intercept any request that starts with `pmtiles://…` and serve the appropriate tile from the PMTiles file. |
| **Source definition** | `type: 'vector'` + `url: 'pmtiles://<name>'` makes MapLibre request tiles from the PMTiles container. |
| **Line layer** | `type: 'line'` + `source-layer` (the name inside the vector tiles) draws the geometry. The `paint` block shows a simple colour/width rule that scales with zoom. |
| **Fit to data (optional)** | When the source finishes loading we can zoom the map to its extent. This works if the PMTiles file includes a `bounds` field (most do). |

---

## 2️⃣  Using a bundler (Vite / webpack / Rollup, etc.)

If you’re building with a bundler, the **only extra step** you need compared to the CDN example is to tell MapLibre where its WebWorker lives:

```ts
// src/main.ts (or .js)
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// Must be called **once**, before any Map instance is created
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
```

Everything else (PMTiles registration, source & layer creation) stays exactly the same as in the CDN snippet.

---

## 3️⃣  Quick checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| ✅ | Import MapLibre GL JS v6 as an ES module (`import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` or `import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl'`). |
| ✅ | Install `@protomaps/pmtiles` (`npm i @protomaps/pmtiles`) if you’re using a bundler. |
| ✅ | Register the PMTiles protocol (`registerProtocol(new PMTiles(url))`). |
| ✅ | Add a vector source with `url: 'pmtiles://<name>'`. |
| ✅ | Add a `line` layer that references the correct `source-layer`. |
| ✅ | (Bundler only) Call `setWorkerUrl()` once before creating the map. |
| ✅ | Pin your CDN versions (`@^6.0.0`) or lock your `package.json` to a specific v6.x release. |

That’s it! Your PMTiles vector tiles will now be rendered as a styled line layer on a MapLibre GL JS v6 map. 🎉

Below is a **complete, minimal example** that

1. loads **MapLibre GL JS v6** (ESM-only) from a CDN,  
2. registers the **PMTiles** protocol,  
3. adds a vector-tile source that reads from a *.pmtiles* file, and  
4. draws a simple line layer from one of the source’s layers.

You can copy-paste the whole thing into an `index.html` file and open it in a browser (or serve it locally with any static-file server).

---

## 1️⃣ HTML + ESM imports

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre GL + PMTiles demo</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 (ESM build) -->
  <script type="module">
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣ Import the libraries
    // -------------------------------------------------
    import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
    import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.js'; // adjust version if needed

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Register the PMTiles protocol
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The PMTiles library creates a custom fetch handler that understands URLs
    // that start with "pmtiles://".  Calling `addProtocol()` registers that handler
    // globally (once per page).
    const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles');
    pmtiles.addProtocol();   // now "pmtiles://your-data.pmtiles" works

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create the map
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},          // we’ll add our source later
        layers: []            // we’ll add our layer later
      },
      center: [0, 0],        // lon, lat
      zoom: 2
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ When the map is ready, add the PMTiles source & line layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.on('load', () => {
      // ---- 4a. Add the vector source ----
      map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL uses the custom protocol we just registered.
        // The part after the double slash is the filename you passed to PMTiles.
        url: 'pmtiles://your-data.pmtiles'
        // If your PMTiles file contains multiple layers you can also
        // specify `maxzoom`, `minzoom`, `attribution`, etc. here.
      });

      // ---- 4b. Add a line layer that draws from a source-layer inside the PMTiles file ----
      // Replace "my_line_layer" with the exact name of the layer you want to render.
      // You can discover the layer names by opening the .pmtiles file in a viewer
      // (e.g. https://github.com/protomaps/PMTilesViewer) or by inspecting the
      // `metadata` property of the PMTiles object.
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-line-layer',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
        'source-layer': 'my_line_layer',   // <-- name of the vector-tile layer
        layout: {
          'line-cap': 'round',
          'line-join': 'round'
        },
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#ff6600',
          'line-width': 3,
          'line-opacity': 0.8
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

### What you need to change

| Placeholder | What to replace | Why |
|-------------|----------------|-----|
| `https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles` | URL of **your** PMTiles file (must be reachable from the browser) | The PMTiles constructor needs the actual file location. |
| `'my_line_layer'` | The exact **source-layer** name inside the PMTiles file that contains the line geometry | Each vector-tile source can contain many layers; you must pick the one you want to style. |
| `center`, `zoom` | Desired initial view | Adjust to focus on the area covered by your tiles. |
| `line-color`, `line-width`, etc. | Styling you prefer | Any valid MapLibre GL line-paint / line-layout properties work. |

---

## 2️⃣ How it works under the hood

1. **PMTiles library** – When you call `pmtiles.addProtocol()`, the library registers a custom `fetch` handler for URLs that start with `pmtiles://`.  
   Internally it reads the `.pmtiles` archive (which is just a single file containing many vector-tile blobs) and serves the requested tile data as if it were coming from a normal HTTP tile server.

2. **MapLibre source definition** – The source is declared as `type: 'vector'` with a `url` that uses the custom protocol. MapLibre will request tiles like `pmtiles://your-data.pmtiles/14/4823/6160.mvt` and the PMTiles handler will return the appropriate MVT blob.

3. **Line layer** – The `source-layer` property tells MapLibre which layer inside each tile to draw. The rest of the layer definition (`paint`, `layout`) is the same as any other MapLibre line layer.

---

## 3️⃣ Bundler note (optional)

If you are **not** loading the libraries from a CDN but instead bundling them with Vite, Webpack, esbuild, etc., you still need to call `setWorkerUrl()` **once** before creating the `Map`. The CDN example above does **not** need that call because the ESM build can resolve the worker automatically.

```js
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
```

Add the snippet above **before** `new Map(...)` if you move the code into a bundler-based project.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick sanity-check checklist

- ✅ The PMTiles file is publicly reachable (CORS headers allow `GET` from your page).  
- ✅ The `pmtiles.addProtocol()` call runs **before** you add the source.  
- ✅ The `source-layer` name matches exactly what’s inside the PMTiles archive.  
- ✅ If you’re using a bundler, you have called `setWorkerUrl()` once.  

When all of the above are satisfied, the map will render the line features from your PMTiles vector tiles just like any other vector source. Happy mapping!

